        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Holding on and bouncing back"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Between 1000 and 1700 CE, new powers entered the subcontinent. Rulers, communities and saints responded with courage, alliances and new ideas."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "Bhakti",
          "Rajputs and Marathas",
          "Sufi",
          "Vijayanagara",
          "culture",
          "economy",
          "resilience",
          "resistance"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Many Indian rulers resisted invasions by defending forts, forming alliances and fighting battles.\nKingdoms like Vijayanagara in the south built strong armies, cities and trade, and supported art and temples.\nRajput rulers and later Maratha leaders defended their lands and ways of life.\nResilience came from local governance, village economies and strong social networks.\nThe Bhakti tradition, with saints like Kabir, Mirabai and Tulsidas, taught devotion and equality and built solidarity among people.\nSufi saints also spread messages of love and harmony.\nAgriculture expanded, crafts and trade grew, and ports linked India with distant markets, keeping the economy sound.\nCultural blending produced new languages, art and architecture."
        }
